export const PASSAGES = {"item:a-bloodstained-journal-page":[["item:a-bloodstained-journal-page#section2","Handwriting and Guilt","Clerk Daltry keeps the registry in Darkshire's Town Hall and knows local handwriting from years of archiving signatures. He compares the page with municipal records and confirms that Stalvan wrote it, leaving no doubt of the tutor's guilt.",["q66.6"]]],"item:a-mysterious-message":[["item:a-mysterious-message#section2","Intercepted Orders","A courier journeying between Defias operations at Moonbrook, the Gold Coast Quarry, and the Jangolode Mine carries the message. Its interception marks a break in Gryan Stoutmantle's investigation. For weeks, the People's Militia suspected someone was directing the bandit uprising, but had no sure proof. The missive confirms that Edwin VanCleef commands the brotherhood's efforts across Westfall through coded ranks and intermediaries.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:arikara-serpent-skin":[["item:arikara-serpent-skin#section2","A Troubling Revelation","At Whitereach Post, Motega Firemane receives the skin as proof that Arikara is dead. Magatha Grimtotem insisted the serpent hunted Cairne Bloodhoof out of ancestral rage. Yet the slain creature leaves Motega bewildered: why would such a spirit threaten their High Chieftain? The pelt settles the immediate danger, but Motega is left questioning Magatha's interpretation.",["q4821.5"]]],"item:ataeric-s-staff":[["item:ataeric-s-staff#section2","A Conduit of Ley Magic","Archmage Ataeric carried the staff to direct ley energies in southern Silverpine Forest. Dalaran sent him to awaken an ancient ley node near the borders of its territory. The staff helped him weave the vast currents around him, making the archmage a grave threat to nearby Forsaken settlements.",["q477.3"]]],"item:athrikus-narassin-s-head":[["item:athrikus-narassin-s-head#section1","Blight Upon Althalaxx","Athrikus Narassin commanded dark sorcerers from the Tower of Althalaxx. The ancient warlock led a coven that practiced fel magic along the Darkshore coast and carried out demonic summonings that threatened the wilderness. Claiming his head breaks the coven's leadership and drives back its immediate foothold in the region, but corruption lingers and will demand future vigilance.",["q954.5"]]],"item:bethor-s-scroll":[["item:bethor-s-scroll#section2","A Colleague's Betrayal","Bethor Iceshard recognizes Thule Ravenclaw's handiwork in tainted ichor recovered from Rot Hide gnolls. Thule was once a promising young mage of the Kirin Tor. When the Scourge rose to power, he betrayed his brethren and served the Lich King. Bethor learns Thule survived and directs Rot Hide attacks across Silverpine Forest from Fenris Isle. Knowing his former colleague's spellcraft well, Bethor writes the scroll.",["q437.6"]]],"item:crude-flint":[["item:crude-flint#section1","Kindling in the Mire","In the Wetlands, Mosshide gnolls use Crude Flint to strike sparks for warmth and to cook meat in the damp bogs. They cut down living timber for their hearths and bonfires. Rethiel the Greenwarden sees each spark as an assault on the marsh's natural balance.",["q463.2"]],["item:crude-flint#section2","The Greenwarden's Retribution","Rethiel the Greenwarden seeks to stop the Mosshide gnolls from cutting down the marsh's trees. He asks passing adventurers to take the firestones from the pack. Rethiel plans to smash each flint to dust and cast the fragments into the sea, so the gnolls can no longer burn the Wetlands' living wood.",["q463.2"]]],"item:dusky-crab-cakes":[["item:dusky-crab-cakes#section1","A Questionable Delicacy","Chef Grual prepares Dusky Crab Cakes for Jitters, a terrified vagrant overcome by hunger. Duskwood offers few fresh provisions, so cooks make do with meat from the forest. Grual’s cakes look like ordinary seafood patties, but their secret ingredient comes from the legs of Duskwood’s monstrous arachnids. Jitters devours the cakes, unaware of what he is eating.",["q5.2"]]],"item:ebonlocke-s-response-to-solomon":[["item:ebonlocke-s-response-to-solomon#section2","The Crumbling Frontier","Solomon is shaken when the letter reaches him. The Stormwind Army has withdrawn from Westfall, and the royal guard is absent from Darkshire. The whole southern frontier has been left to decay. Solomon sees a terrible pattern in Stormwind's neglect of its outlying lands, and suspects treachery among the kingdom's highest powers.",["q120.5"]]],"item:fel-cone":[["item:fel-cone#section2","A Bitter Medicine","Zenn Foulhoof is a satyr who tricks passersby into doing his bidding. When travelers fall for his wiles, Syral Bladeleaf and the Sentinels direct them to gather Fel Cones and offer them to Foulhoof as an innocent snack. The corruption in a cone briefly transforms the satyr into a harmless frog.",["q488.2"]]],"item:filled-etched-phial":[["item:filled-etched-phial#section2","A Key to Centaur Sorcery","Zangen Stonehoof and Rau Cliffrunner sought this water for the deep ties between dryads and centaur. Both trace their ancestry to Cenarius, and their magic shares a primal resonance. In Thousand Needles, the Galak clan guards an ancient relic behind an unyielding protective flame said to date from the creation of the centaur race. The moonwell water in the dryad phial can pierce and extinguish that ward.",["q1195.3"]]],"item:gem-of-anacondra":[["item:gem-of-anacondra#section2","Cleansing the Vision","Nara Wildmane and the circle of Cenarion druids in Thunder Bluff found their own slumbers plagued by the visions and wicked faces of the Fang's leadership. Claiming the Gem of Anacondra—alongside the stones of Cobrahn, Pythas, and Serpentis—is necessary to verify that Lady Anacondra has been struck down, lifting the oppressive nightmare from the minds of Kalimdor's balance-keepers.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gem-of-cobrahn":[["item:gem-of-cobrahn#section2","Restoring Balance","In Thunder Bluff, the tauren druid Nara Wildmane is tormented by nightmares sent by the rogue Druids of the Fang. To cleanse the subterranean springs and protect Kalimdor from their vision, adventurers must delve into the caverns, slay Lord Cobrahn alongside his three conspirators, and recover their dream gems. Returning Cobrahn's gem to the Cenarion druids brings an end to his corrupting influence over the slumbering Naralex.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gem-of-serpentis":[["item:gem-of-serpentis#section2","Proof for Thunder Bluff","The tauren druids atop Thunder Bluff's Elder Rise, particularly Nara Wildmane, endure vivid nightmares caused by the corruption lurking beneath the savannah. Recovering the Gem of Serpentis alongside those of Cobrahn, Anacondra, and Pythas provides tangible proof that the rogue order's ringleaders have fallen, easing the nightmare's grasp on the Cenarion Circle in Mulgore.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gnoll-paw":[["item:gnoll-paw#section2","A Coordinated Menace","Riverpaw gnolls in Westfall forage across the plains, but militia observers have noticed signs of close coordination with Defias thieves based around the Deadmines. The packs terrorize farmsteads and prey on supply wagons, helping shield the bandits from local resistance. Bringing in gnoll paws helps keep the overland routes between Sentinel Hill and Elwynn Forest open.",["defias.hideout"]]],"item:gunther-s-spellbook":[["item:gunther-s-spellbook#section1","A Token of Old Kinship","Gunther Arcanus once studied magic in Dalaran with Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw as fellow Kirin Tor members. When the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron, Gunther fell and rose again as one of the undead. In time, he broke free of the Scourge's hold on his mind. Believing he stood alone and that the Lich King's agents hunted him, Gunther grew deeply hostile to outsiders. After the tome was recovered, Bethor laid an arcane enchantment across its pages. Its magic bears Bethor's signature, so Gunther can recognize his old companion's presence and learn that another mage has kept his own will.",["q358.4"]]],"item:head-of-vancleef":[["item:head-of-vancleef#section2","A Grim Victory","VanCleef's death answers Stoutmantle's call for Westfall's good, but the sight of his head carries solemn weight. He was once the revered master builder who rebuilt Stormwind after the First War. Betrayal and unpaid debts drove him and the Stonemasons Guild into outlaw rebellion. His death marks the end of the local militia's campaign and exposes the kingdom's failure to settle its own domestic turmoil. Volunteer farmers and foreign adventurers have been left to carry out high justice.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:incendia-powder":[["item:incendia-powder#section2","Uneasy Portents","Magatha Grimtotem shaped the rites behind Incendia Powder. She believed changes in tauren ways had stirred ancient spirits to hunt High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, so she enchanted the powder to draw the apparition into the open and destroy it. The powder brings forth the fierce serpent, but the encounter leaves questions about why Arikara was stirred.",["q4821.5"]]],"item:jitters-completed-journal":[["item:jitters-completed-journal#section2","Unraveling the Darkness","The journal leads Sven to share what he has seen: black-cloaked riders speaking with the wizard Morbent Fel at the Forlorn Rowe. Sven sends the manuscript north with travelers to the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind. Bishop Farthing reads Jitters' account to learn about the vile taint in Duskwood, then uses its revelations to help craft a holy weapon that can pierce Fel's dark wards.",["q164.5"]]],"item:letter-to-jin-zil":[["item:letter-to-jin-zil#section2","Old Memories in Malaka'jin","At the cave in Malaka'jin, Jin'Zil receives the news with nostalgic satisfaction. Serena's death recalls the founding of Durotar after the Third War, when Rokhan, Jin'Zil's friend, joined Rexxar, a half-ogre beastmaster, to kill Serena's elder sister, Bloodfeather. Serena has fallen too, and Jin'Zil believes the Bloodfeather line's vengeance is ended.",["q867.3"]]],"item:lieutenant-s-insignia":[["item:lieutenant-s-insignia#section2","The Cleft of Shadow","Warchief Thrall sends his agent to the Cleft of Shadow with Lieutenant's Insignia, to present it to Neeru Fireblade. Neeru mistakes the agent for a newly promoted subordinate. Confident in his standing, the warlock boasts that the Burning Blade serves the Shadow Council and the Burning Legion. His words give Thrall a way to expose the corruption beneath the city.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]]],"item:nether-gem":[["item:nether-gem#section1","A Conduit of Arcane Communion","Gunther Arcanus forged or enchanted the Nether Gem on his solitary island in Brightwater Lake. He had broken free from the Lich King's control by his own will, yet suspicion kept him apart from the young Forsaken nation. After his lost spellbook is returned, Gunther gives the gem to an emissary as a gesture of goodwill. Atop the Magic Quarter in the Undercity, Bethor Iceshard channels magic through the stone. Gunther's magical visage appears before the Mage Guild leader, letting the two mages speak across Tirisfal Glades. Their meeting opens a path for Gunther to align with Lady Sylvanas and the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"item:nightsaber-fang":[["item:nightsaber-fang#section2","A Satyr's Reagent","Outside Dolanaar, the satyr Zenn Foulhoof preys on eager, inexperienced travelers. He claims to need woodland reagents and persuades newcomers to gather nightsaber fangs, strigid feathers, and spider silk. Giving him these materials aids the trickster, who openly mocks the naive hero when they deliver them.",["q488.2"]]],"item:pendant-of-shadow":[["item:pendant-of-shadow#section1","Token of a Dark Warlock","Morganth, a rogue warlock, holds the Pendant of Shadow at the Tower of Ilgalar. He sought the mysteries of the Scythe of Elune, an ancient relic of immense and volatile power. Theocritus feared the havoc Morganth might unleash upon the Redridge Mountains and the kingdom of Stormwind. The mage sent adventurers to infiltrate Ilgalar, end Morganth's plotting, and take the talisman as proof of his demise.",["q94.4"]]],"item:pyrewood-shackle":[["item:pyrewood-shackle#section1","Arugal's Binding Magic","Pyrewood's residents live ordinary lives by day, appearing as humans, then become feral Moonrage worgen at sunset. Archmage Arugal bound the villagers in enchanted shackles as part of his experiments with worgen sorcery. At the Sepulcher, Forsaken arcane researcher Dalar Dawnweaver studies the bands to learn what their enchantments do. He gathers and examines the lingering energy on them, hoping to understand Arugal's magic and loosen the mad archmage's hold over the region.",["q421.5"]]],"item:red-leather-bandana":[["item:red-leather-bandana#section1","Mark of the Brotherhood","Across Westfall’s fields and abandoned farms, highwaymen, looters, and rebellious laborers wear crimson bandanas to hide their faces. The People's Militia at Sentinel Hill sees each one as proof of loyalty to Edwin VanCleef's rogue fraternity. Scout Galiaan collects the bandanas from travelers to follow the Brotherhood’s movements and dwindling numbers across the region.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:resonite-crystal":[["item:resonite-crystal#section1","Ancient Resonance","Resonite Crystal has drawn kobolds into frantic digging deep in Boulderslide Ravine. Their sudden focus on these stones alarms the shamans at Sun Rock Retreat. Mor'rogal examines the crystals and finds a faint magical hum. He recognizes traces of Earthen magic, signs that the kobolds have broken into an ancient layer shaped by the Titans.",["q6421.2"]],["item:resonite-crystal#section2","Tapping the Earth","Resonite Crystal shows that kobold diggers have reached deep enough to disturb the ancient Earthen. The Titans shaped the Earthen to carve and mold the foundations of the world, and their magic still clings to the surrounding rock. Horde agents gather the crystals to learn what drives the kobolds and turn the enchanted stone's power against whatever slumbers below.",["q6421.2"]]],"item:sarilus-foulborne-s-head":[["item:sarilus-foulborne-s-head#section1","A Defiler Silenced","Sarilus Foulborne was a Forsaken spellcaster atop Dreadmist Peak in the northern Barrens. He drew the night elves' anger by corrupting and binding swarms of water elementals. From Silverwind Refuge, Sentinel Velene Starstrike recognized his work and sent champions across the frontier into Horde territory to end his experiments. Starstrike would accept only his severed head as proof that the mocking mage was dead, and that the troubled waters of Ashenvale might know some peace.",["q1016.2"]]],"item:sealed-description-of-thredd-s-visitor":[["item:sealed-description-of-thredd-s-visitor#section2","From Stonemasons to Spies","City architect Baros Alexston found no clear match in the description, though he suspected the visitor might be tied to the Defias rebellion. Alexston sent the sealed envelope to Master Mathias Shaw, who leads Stormwind's intelligence service, SI:7. Shaw recognized the man as a lethal mercenary who uses names including Marzon and now does dirty work for Lord Gregor Lescovar.",["q373.5"]]],"item:serena-s-head":[["item:serena-s-head#section1","A Warning on the Road","Serena Bloodfeather led the Witchwing harpies of the Dry Hills in attacks on trade through the northern Barrens. Her assaults on Horde supply lines grew vicious because her sister had fallen to the beastmaster Rexxar. Darsok Swiftdagger, an orc rogue, ordered Serena hunted to break the flock's morale. He wants her severed head mounted on an outbound caravan, where harpies watching from the crags will see the warning.",["q867.3"]]],"item:shaw-s-report":[["item:shaw-s-report#section1","Intelligence on the Stonemasons","Shaw's Report reaches Sentinel Hill from SI:7's covert headquarters in Stormwind. The dispatch gives the People's Militia its first clear account of who commands the bandits ravaging Westfall. Under Edwin VanCleef, the Stonemasons Guild painstakingly rebuilt Stormwind City after its destruction in the First War. When the crown and nobility refused to pay the masons fairly for their work, resentment took root. Shaw's intelligence links the displaced craftsmen to the criminal uprising now seizing the kingdom's outer territories.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:strigid-owl-feather":[["item:strigid-owl-feather#section1","Forest Plumage and Dark Dealings","Strigid owls and screechers hunt through the Crown of the Earth. Their feathers and down lie across the forest floor. Outside Dolanaar, the renegade satyr Zenn Foulhoof sends inexperienced travelers down the road from Shadowglen to gather strigid feathers, spider silk, and nightsaber fangs for his shady business.",["q488.2"]]],"item:the-lich-s-spellbook":[["item:the-lich-s-spellbook#section2","Unmasking Gunther Arcanus","The recovered spellbook reveals that the reclusive lich is Gunther Arcanus, Bethor Iceshard's old friend and comrade-in-arms from before the Third War. Gunther was a necromantic prodigy in life, and his writings show that his power grew greater in undeath. Bethor knows Gunther could be a valued master of the dark arts for the Forsaken. Someone must first convince the suspicious lich that the Forsaken are free of the Lich King's grip.",["q358.4"]]],"item:thule-s-head":[["item:thule-s-head#section1","Trophy of Fenris Isle","Fenris Isle is the Lich King's chief Scourge stronghold in Silverpine Forest. From Fenris Keep, the necromancer Thule Ravenclaw commands rot hides and undead minions that threaten Forsaken outposts, including the Sepulcher. High Executor Hadrec will accept no report of Thule's death without his severed head. When Thule dies, dark magic surges through the woods. His head gives Hadrec the proof he needs to declare Fenris Isle broken.",["q437.6"]]],"item:translated-letter":[["item:translated-letter#section1","A Gruesome Revelation","The errands began as acts of mercy for a seemingly helpless hermit in Raven Hill. Abercrombie's obscure message to Mayor Ello Ebonlocke remained a mystery until Sirra Von'Indi translated it. The words reveal that the hermit is the Embalmer. He used the supplies brought to him to build Stitches, a monstrous abomination, then unleashed the hulking creation on Darkshire's people to trample and slaughter them.",["q165.5"]]],"item:whitestone-oak-lumber":[["item:whitestone-oak-lumber#section2","Timber of the Under-Docks","Deep in the Deadmines beneath Moonbrook, Goblin Woodcarvers shape Whitestone Oak for the Defias Brotherhood's hidden war fleet. The dense grain bears strain and hard blows, making it useful to armorers who need stronger timber than common ash or pine for ceremonial and martial work.",["defias.hideout"]]],"item:wiley-s-note":[["item:wiley-s-note#section2","The Stonemason Connection","Beyond noting mercenary pacts, Wiley lets slip a crucial reference connecting the Defias syndicate to the Stonemasons Guild who rebuilt Stormwind after the Second War. Sensing the explosive political implications of this detail, Stoutmantle redirects the note directly into the hands of Master Mathias Shaw, spymaster of SI:7, pulling Stormwind's intelligence apparatus directly into the investigation.",["q123.3"]]],"item:woven-wand":[["item:woven-wand#section2","A Sundered Bond","When Dalaran still trained the young magi of the north, apprentices Bethor Iceshard, Gunther Arcanus, and Thule Ravenclaw joined their wands into one talisman. The Woven Wand focused their combined magical power and marked their friendship. The Scourge's march through Lordaeron broke that bond. Thule swore fealty to the Lich King and turned to necromancy. The Plague of Undeath claimed Bethor and Gunther, severing their ties to Dalaran.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:abercrombie":[["npc:abercrombie#section2","The Embalmer's Madness","Abercrombie once lived in Raven Hill as a respected, kindly alchemist. When his beloved wife, Eliza, died, grief broke his sanity. He turned to forbidden necromantic arts, cut out his own heart, and placed it in Eliza's dead breast to bring her back. The ritual made her a monster with an insatiable hunger for living flesh, and the mad alchemist kept her buried in the cemetery.",["q165.5"]],["npc:abercrombie#section3","A Monstrous Deception","Abercrombie, known in dark folklore as the Embalmer, nurses a deep, psychotic grudge against Darkshire and its leaders. He sends helpful travelers for ghoul ribs, specialty thread, and caustic brews. Those supplies are the final parts needed to assemble Stitches, a colossal abomination stitched from flesh and built to slaughter the Night Watch and ravage the town.",["q165.5"]]],"npc:arugal":[["npc:arugal#section3","The Scourge of Silverpine","From Shadowfang Keep, Arugal's power reaches across the surrounding countryside. His dark sorcery blankets Pyrewood Village, where townsfolk live under a nocturnal curse, and fills the Deep Elem Mine with ravenous Moonrage beasts. The Forsaken see him as a threat they cannot tolerate. Sylvanas Windrunner's Deathstalkers and apothecaries plot the mad archmage's execution to secure the borders of their fledgling kingdom.",["q421.5"]]],"npc:asterion":[["npc:asterion#section3","Vengeance from the Grave","Though vile satyrs and grells roam the ruins of Bashal'Aran feeding on residual arcane power, they shun the shrine where Asterion's ghost lingers. If an Alliance adventurer retrieves the moonstone seal from the satyrs and shatters it in Ameth'Aran, Asterion's spectral chains finally break. Yet freedom does not bring him peace; sensing that Athrikus Narassin still endures, the ancient mage turns his thoughts from ten thousand years of despair toward vengeance.",["q954.5"]]],"npc:baros-alexston":[["npc:baros-alexston#section2","A Bitter Parting of Ways","When Stormwind's rebuilding ended, the House of Nobles refused the agreed payment. Furious protests followed, the Stonemasons Guild dissolved, and deadly riots filled the streets. Edwin VanCleef led the unpaid artisans into exile, where they formed the Defias Brotherhood. The nobles offered Baros the post of City Architect if he disavowed the uprising. Baros disagreed with the guild's turn toward violent banditry and accepted, leaving a bitter rift between himself and his former comrades.",["defias.masons"]],["npc:baros-alexston#section3","The Ghost in the Dead Mines","Baros oversees the capital's upkeep from his desk in City Hall, and Mathias Shaw of SI:7 discreetly owes him a favor. After VanCleef fell in the depths of the Deadmines, an unsent letter addressed to Baros remained on the Defias leader's corpse. Its presence recalls the bond broken by politics, treason, and bloodshed over Stormwind's foundations.",["defias.hideout"]]],"npc:bethor-iceshard":[["npc:bethor-iceshard#section2","Old Friends and Bittersweet Reunions","Bethor Iceshard still remembers his former companions. He tracks powerful undead spellcasters across Tirisfal and Silverpine, sending agents to learn whether rogue practitioners serve the Lich King or might join the Forsaken cause. When he finds Gunther Arcanus raising the dead at Brightwater Lake, Bethor patiently works to bring his old friend into Lady Sylvanas's fold. Thule Ravenclaw receives no such mercy; his willing betrayal doomed their brotherhood.",["q358.4"]],["npc:bethor-iceshard#section3","Bonds of Apprenticehood","Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, Bethor Iceshard studied magic as a mortal apprentice beside his close companions, Gunther Arcanus and Thule Ravenclaw. The three wove their practice wands together to mark their fellowship. The Scourge shattered that bond. As death and dark magic swept the kingdom, Thule willingly gave his soul to the Lich King. Gunther perished and rose again on an isolated island in Brightwater Lake. Bethor succumbed as well, then broke free as Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:brainwashed-noble":[["npc:brainwashed-noble#section1","A Pawn of the Brotherhood","Brainwashed Noble wanders the passages leading into the Deadmines, among bandits and rotting corpses. Remnants of highborn finery suggest he belonged to Stormwind's House of Nobles, perhaps as a sitting member or a scion. Edwin VanCleef took him captive, and the Defias chose enchantments over ransom, bending his mind into that of a spellcasting guard for their sanctuary. The Brotherhood's hatred of the peers who refused to pay after the city was rebuilt reaches even into the caverns beneath Westfall.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"npc:chef-grual":[["npc:chef-grual#section2","Secret Ingredients","Chef Grual is known for Dusky Crab Cakes and seasoned wolf kabobs. Duskwood lies far from the coast, so his crab cakes contain gooey legs from the local Venom Web Spiders. Whether patrons know what they are eating or prefer not to ask, Grual keeps the kitchen running without apology.",["q5.2"]]],"npc:deathstalker-faerleia":[["npc:deathstalker-faerleia#section1","Behind Enemy Lines","Deathstalker Faerleia works from the upper levels of Pyrewood Village's town hall. As an agent of the Deathstalkers, the Undercity's covert intelligence and assassin branch, she has infiltrated the isolated human enclave in southern Silverpine Forest. The village holds dark secrets, including a curse that transforms its people beneath the night sky. From inside the settlement, Faerleia coordinates operations to disrupt it and calls on Horde operatives to eliminate influential members of the Pyrewood Council.",["q421.5"]]],"npc:ebru":[["npc:ebru#section2","The Price of Corruption","The nightmare twisted not only the ecosystem of the caves, but also Ebru's fellow disciples, turning them into the fanatical Druids of the Fang. Stripped of her companionship and facing the ruin of her circle, Ebru focuses on containment.",["q886.5","q886.6"]]],"npc:edwin-vancleef":[["npc:edwin-vancleef#section3","Retribution from the Shadows","The Defias drove Westfall's farmers from their homes and claimed its rich gold deposits while the crown neglected the countryside. Deep in Moonbrook's Deadmines, VanCleef directs the building of a massive juggernaut warship for an armed assault on Stormwind. The House of Nobles dismisses the thieves as disorganized brigands, while VanCleef has joined forces with river crooks, gnolls, and pirates. His embittered labor union has become an army poised against the monarchy.",["defias.juggernaut"]]],"npc:foreman-thistlenettle":[["npc:foreman-thistlenettle#section2","Tragedy in the Deadmines","Before the Defias Brotherhood took full control of the Deadmines, mining crews worked its rich veins. Foreman Thistlenettle led one such expedition for the Miners' League and the Explorers' League. Deep beneath Moonbrook, a violent cave-in sealed his party away from the surface. The miners died in the darkness, but did not remain at rest. A necrotic taint raised Thistlenettle and his crew as ravenous ghouls, stalking the shadowed passages.",["defias.hideout"]]],"npc:gelkak-gyromast":[["npc:gelkak-gyromast#section2","The Threshwackonator Malfunction","Once travelers recover the key, Gelkak sends them to switch on the Threshwackonator 4100 and lead it back to him. He hopes to set it against the murlocs and beasts along the coast. The machine follows obediently, then malfunctions and turns hostile as it reaches its captain. The travelers must destroy it.",["q2098.3"]]],"npc:glorin-steelbrow":[["npc:glorin-steelbrow#section2","The Doomed Fleet and Lost Relics","Glorin Steelbrow knows the tragedy of the Third Fleet, whose ships were battered and dragged beneath the waves along the Wetlands shore. He guides travelers through the haunted wreckage in search of lost ingots of Lightforge iron and the powerful, cursed artifact called the Eye of Paleth. Glorin knows these holy arms are vital to forging weapons that can hold back the dark forces threatening the Alliance.",["q288.4"]]],"npc:gryan-stoutmantle":[["npc:gryan-stoutmantle#section3","Unmasking the Kingpin","Gryan Stoutmantle sought to learn who was directing Westfall's ruin. He called in an old debt from Wiley the Black, a rogue in Redridge, and conferred with Mathias Shaw of SI:7. Their intelligence revealed that Edwin VanCleef led the bandits. VanCleef was the slighted master architect of the Stonemasons' Guild. After the militia captured a bandit trying to plunder a local wagon, Gryan used the prisoner to find the Brotherhood's headquarters beneath Moonbrook, within the Deadmines. He sent veteran champions to strike VanCleef directly.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"npc:gunther-arcanus":[["npc:gunther-arcanus#section2","The Prodigal Lich","The Forsaken sought powerful allies against the Scourge, so they sent emissaries to claim Gunther's spellbook. In the Undercity, Bethor Iceshard recognized the handwriting as that of his long-lost peer. Gunther enchanted the returned grimoire and demanded the destruction of local Scourge commanders. Through this, he learned that others had broken free. Convinced that Sylvanas's faction truly rebels against the Scourge, Gunther has made a pact with his former schoolmate to aid the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]],["npc:gunther-arcanus#section3","A Student of Dalaran","Before Lordaeron fell, Gunther studied the arcane arts as a promising Kirin Tor apprentice in Dalaran. With close friends Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw, he secretly pursued necromancy despite the Kirin Tor's strict ban on dark magic. To mark their brotherhood, the three bound their apprentice wands together as one heirloom.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:kam-deepfury":[["npc:kam-deepfury#section3","A Score to Settle","Motley Garmason and the grieving Longbraid the Grim seek mercenaries in the Wetlands to reach Kam Deepfury in Stormwind's riot-torn Stockade. The Dark Iron insurrectionist has found brief shelter from his victims' relatives in prison. Those seeking him want his head, to settle a bloody grudge begun hundreds of miles to the north.",["q303.3"]]],"npc:lord-serpentis":[["npc:lord-serpentis#section3","The Final Obstacle","Perched deep inside the winding caverns, Lord Serpentis is one of the four leaders of the rogue sect, each of whom carries a dream gem. Those seeking to wake the trapped master or reclaim the waters of the Oasis must strike down Serpentis and claim the gem he carries, breaking the serpent's hold over the labyrinth.",["q886.6"]]],"npc:mathias-shaw":[["npc:mathias-shaw#section2","Shadow of VanCleef","Mathias Shaw once called Edwin VanCleef a childhood companion. Shaw trained VanCleef in stealth and assassination, hoping the gifted stonemason would serve the kingdom beside him. But after the Stonemasons Guild was cast out following Stormwind's rebuilding, VanCleef turned those lessons against the crown. Shaw now directs surveillance and infiltration across Westfall to break his old friend's rebellion.",["defias.masons"]]],"npc:morgan-the-collector":[["npc:morgan-the-collector#section2","A Signet of the Past","Morgan carries a distinctive signet ring that marks his authority. Investigators in Goldshire see the ring as evidence connecting the masked thieves terrorizing the heartland to the disgraced Stonemasons Guild, which rebuilt Stormwind City after the Second War. Recovering the ring reveals who directs the bandits in the woods.",["q123.3"]]],"npc:naralex":[["npc:naralex#section3","Fallen Disciples","The nightmare did not stop with the wildlife. Naralex's own disciples, driven completely mad by the psychic backlash bleeding out of their master's trance, broke away from the traditions of the Cenarion Circle. Casting aside their reverence for balance, they took on serpent forms and renamed themselves the Druids of the Fang. Believing their twisted growth must spread across Kalimdor, they now guard Naralex's slumbering body to ensure he never wakes, leaving only a few loyal disciples searching for allies brave enough to break the trance.",["q886.5"]]],"npc:orgnil-soulscar":[["npc:orgnil-soulscar#section2","Hunting the Shadow Council's Remnants","Orgnil Soulscar hunts the Burning Blade cult, whose warlocks hide in caves such as Skull Rock. Fel magic devastated the orcish people in past generations, and he will not let demon worship gain ground near Orgrimmar. He sends trusted recruits against cultists to gather ritual talismans. He also sends them to consult local hermits and shamans such as Margoz about the dark magic at work.",["q5726.3"]]],"npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden":[["npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden#section2","Guardian of the Marsh","Rethiel watches over the marsh from the damp thickets of the Green Belt. His towering form is made of moss, peat, and primal plant life. Unlike the bog beasts that wander the marsh as aggressive predators, Rethiel is conscious and speaks with sorrow. He feels the Wetlands as a living ecosystem, suffering under foreign predation and corruption.",["q463.2"]]],"npc:sven-yorgen":[["npc:sven-yorgen#section3","Uncovering the Truth","Jitters, an errant vagrant, was the shadowy figure stalking Sven's property. His blundering drew the Dark Riders into Duskwood as they searched for the Scythe of Elune. Jitters did not strike the fatal blows, but his actions unleashed the nightmare that destroyed the Yorgen family. Sven's fury now turns toward the dark forces haunting Raven Hill and the necromancer Morbent Fel.",["q164.5"]]],"npc:the-defias-traitor":[["npc:the-defias-traitor#section1","Leading the Way to Moonbrook","Under guard at Sentinel Hill's watchtower, the traitor must lead Alliance champions through hostile territory to the hidden entrance beneath ruined Moonbrook. His former comrades hate him, and his captors despise him. Yet his betrayal gives the People's Militia the opening it needs to strike at the Brotherhood's heart.",["defias.hideout"]],["npc:the-defias-traitor#section2","A Bargain for Survival","The Defias Brotherhood does not command every member's loyalty to Edwin VanCleef. Gryan Stoutmantle's militia caught the petty thief trying to steal Farmer Saldean's wagon. Facing execution, he chose to save himself. He gave the militia the exact location of the Brotherhood's hidden base in exchange for his life.",["defias.masons"]]],"npc:verdan-the-everliving":[["npc:verdan-the-everliving#section2","Twisted by the Nightmare","When Naralex attempted to cultivate the dry Barrens into a lush paradise, his ritual fractured into a vivid nightmare within the Emerald Dream. The resulting corruption bled through the subterranean waters, tainting everything living inside the cavern. Amid that blight, Verdan roams as an enormous, hostile brute in the depths shared with the mad Druids of the Fang.",["q886.5"]]],"npc:zenn-foulhoof":[["npc:zenn-foulhoof#section1","A Schemer in the Woods","Zenn Foulhoof keeps a place just outside Dolanaar, where the night elves tolerate him under watch. Most satyrs serve demonic masters and attack openly, but Zenn offers a friendly face. He says he has changed, or asks to trade for nightsaber fangs, strigid owl feathers, and webwood spider silk. Gullible youths gather the ingredients he needs for foul rituals. Dolanaar's council will not let him act unchecked. They order travelers to feed him corrupted forest fruit, humiliating him by turning the scheming satyr into a harmless frog for a time.",["q488.2"]]],"subzone:ambermill":[["subzone:ambermill#section2","Tapping the Ley Lines","Ambermill draws the Kirin Tor for more than defense. The village lies over a dormant ley line energy node. Forsaken spies and Deathstalkers watching the town have learned that Dalaran's magi are trying to awaken the nexus for large-scale arcane work. The surge could threaten the Forsaken's fragile hold on Silverpine Forest. Shadow Priest Allister and the Deathstalkers are working to stop them through sabotage, stolen Kirin Tor documents, and assassinations across the mill grounds.",["q477.3"]]],"subzone:ameth-aran":[["subzone:ameth-aran#section2","Betrayal and Eternal Torment","As the world began to tear apart, Athrikus Narassin returned to Ameth'Aran and promised his panicked subjects salvation through powerful shielding magic. Instead, he stole their life force to feed his own soul gems, disguising the betrayal as a promise of eternal preservation. Dark magic and a great cataclysm shattered Ameth'Aran. Anguished Highborne specters still stalk its grounds, unable to rest ten thousand years after their murder.",["q965.3"]]],"subzone:boulderslide-cavern":[["subzone:boulderslide-cavern#section2","The Slumbering Giant","Goggeroc lies asleep inside a resonite cask deep in Boulderslide Cavern. The kobolds' digging has reached this ancient earthen, and their work around his resting place risks waking him. His primordial stone magic could bring sudden danger to the whole ravine.",["q6421.2"]]],"subzone:brightwater-lake":[["subzone:brightwater-lake#section2","The Hermit's Refuge","Rising from the center of Brightwater Lake is an island known as Gunther's Retreat. Gunther Arcanus broke free of the Lich King's control on his own, but believed all other undead remained Scourge slaves. He isolated himself and set undead servants to guard his retreat. Bethor Iceshard seeks to show his old friend that the Forsaken also possess free will. After an adventurer proves their opposition to the Scourge, Gunther agrees to join the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle":[["subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle#section2","Schemes on the Mesas","Commanded by leaders such as Arnak Grimtotem and Harb Foulmountain, the settlement is more than a simple outpost. Secret correspondence discovered on the mesa suggests the Grimtotem are actively coordinating with dark and unknown powers, pursuing ambitions far removed from the peace forged by Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff.",["q4542.4"]]],"subzone:dead-field":[["subzone:dead-field#section2","Ravenclaw's Ambition","Thule Ravenclaw directs the Rot Hide gnolls from Fenris Isle. The necromancer seeks to establish Scourge dominance over Silverpine and drive Sylvanas's forces from Lordaeron. He sends magical apparitions to the farmstead to survey his undead horde and warn Forsaken intruders.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:deadmines":[["subzone:deadmines#section2","VanCleef's Foundry","After Stormwind's stonemasons rioted, the newly formed Defias Brotherhood took the Deadmines as its chief refuge. Stormwind's Miners' League tried to reopen the tunnels under Foreman Thistlenettle, but Defias brigands ambushed the workers and collapsed the shafts. The slain miners now wander as restless undead. Ogre enforcers and goblin tinkerers guard the deep tunnels, where the Defias have built an industrial war camp around a shipyard in Ironclad Cove.",["defias.masons"]]],"subzone:defias-hideout":[["subzone:defias-hideout#section1","A Gateway in Ruin","The Defias Hideout hides Edwin VanCleef’s southern operations beneath a ruined house in Moonbrook. Vagrants and Defias thugs move through its ransacked rooms, much like those of any other manor or farmhouse in the settlement. On the upper floor, an opening leads down into the winding passages of the Deadmines. The Brotherhood keeps most of its strength around Moonbrook’s perimeter and at work underground, so few guards remain inside. They watch the hideout as a narrow passage into the mines, not as a stronghold of its own.",["defias.hideout"]]],"subzone:dreadmist-peak":[["subzone:dreadmist-peak#section2","A Convergence of Schemes","Dreadmist Peak draws more than the Burning Blade. Sarilus Foulborne, a rogue Forsaken mage, has made camp on its heights. He bends elemental forces to corrupt water spirits as far north as Ashenvale.",["q1016.2"]]],"subzone:fenris-isle":[["subzone:fenris-isle#section2","The Master of Fenris Keep","Thule Ravenclaw commands Fenris Keep from its crumbling stone halls. Once aligned with the Kirin Tor, the necromancer turned to the Scourge’s dark arts and bent the native gnolls to his will. His sorcery raises their fallen kin and fortifies the island against intrusion. From this isolated sanctuary, Thule oversees the lake’s ferry crossings. Forsaken scouts and Horde forces strike ahead of any greater threat, seeking to break his hold on the archipelago.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:fenris-keep":[["subzone:fenris-keep#section1","The Scourge on Lordamere","Today, Fenris Keep serves as the seat of Ravenclaw's power and an imminent threat to the Forsaken securing Silverpine Forest. From this bastion, Thule directs legions of Rot Hide gnolls that violently assail anyone daring to set foot on the isle. Because Fenris Isle sits mere miles across the water from the Tirisfal border, the Forsaken of the Sepulcher and Undercity actively dispatch champions to batter down the keep's defenses and put an end to the rogue necromancer.",["q437.6"]],["subzone:fenris-keep#section2","A Fallen Bastion of Lordaeron","Before the Third War, Fenris Keep held strategic importance for the Kingdom of Lordaeron, stationing a sizable contingent of troops to safeguard Lordamere Lake. When the undead Scourge swept across the land, the island fortress was overwhelmed along with the rest of the realm. Rather than crumbling into forgotten ruin, it was seized by Thule Ravenclaw, a traitorous former wizard of the Kirin Tor. Ravenclaw allied himself with the Scourge, subjugated the resident gnolls with foul necromancy to create the ravenous Rot Hide pack, and entrenched himself deep inside the keep's stone halls.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:lordamere-lake":[["subzone:lordamere-lake#section2","A Contested Expanse","Dark waters make travel across Lordamere Lake perilous. Fenris Isle lies at its center, where the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw and his Rot Hide gnolls hold out against the Forsaken. Farther east, aggressive Vile Fin murlocs overrun the Dawning Isles. From the ruins around Dalaran to the shoreline of the Undercity, the lake is now a drowned frontier contested by remnant monsters and the armies of the dead.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:lost-fleet":[["subzone:lost-fleet#section2","The Curse of Paleth","The sailors of the Third Fleet did not find rest in their watery grave. Deep in the Intrepid’s sunken cargo hold lay the Cursed Eye of Paleth, a forbidden artifact hoarded by the ship’s greedy commander, Captain Halyndor. The relic corrupted the drowned crews and bound their spirits to the broken timbers as raving, cursed undead. Only the ghost of First Mate Snellig retains any semblance of sanity. He wanders the marshy shore while his former crewmates lash out violently at anyone who comes near the wrecks.",["q288.4"]]],"subzone:lost-point":[["subzone:lost-point#section2","Den of Deserters","The deserters ensconced at Lost Point answer to Balos Jacken, a disgruntled former soldier who has banded these rogues together. Their presence is far more than a simple matter of military insubordination. Jacken and his cohorts hold key testimonies regarding traitorous elements within Theramore's ranks, including the involvement of Private Hendel in a high-stakes conspiracy that reaches into the upper echelons of the Alliance.",["q1274.5"]]],"subzone:mannoroc-coven":[["subzone:mannoroc-coven#section1","A Bastion of Fel Fire","Situated south of Ghost Walker Post and east of Shadowprey Village, Mannoroc Coven blankets the ancient night elf ruins in an oppressive, fel-tainted pall. The coven is commanded on the ground by Lord Azrethoc, alongside the Burning Blade warlock Jugkar Grim'rod. Together, they harness active summoning portals to pull demonic reinforcements straight into Kalimdor, including doomguards, infernals, and felhounds. Even as Horde-aligned travelers attempt to sever these links using relics like the Hand of Iruxos, new rifts continue to tear open through the scarred earth.",["q1480.4"]]],"subzone:manor-mistmantle":[["subzone:manor-mistmantle#section2","The Master of the Estate","Stalvan Mistmantle, once a schoolteacher and private tutor, now acts as an agent of the undead. His horrific crimes against the family of his student became dark legend across the kingdom. He lurks on the upper floor of his family home, among remnants of his slaughtered victims, with shambling horrors guarding him. Travelers and investigators from Darkshire often find that the trail of Duskwood's most notorious phantom ends at this blood-soaked manor.",["q66.7"]]],"subzone:moonbrook":[["subzone:moonbrook#section2","Defias Stronghold","Moonbrook’s streets and ransacked homes are patrolled by red-masked thugs and rogue wizards. Harvest golems roam the fields around town. At the edge of Moonbrook, an unassuming barn hides an entrance to the Deadmines beneath it. The Brotherhood guards the town above its tunnels, where rebellion against Stormwind is being prepared.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"subzone:olsen-s-farthing":[["subzone:olsen-s-farthing#section2","The Grasp of Ravenclaw","Thule Ravenclaw commands the undead haunting Olsen's Farthing. The powerful necromancer works from Fenris Isle, at the center of Lake Lordamere, where dark sorcery lets him raise and enslave the dead from the surrounding countryside. His servants reach across Olsen's Farthing and threaten the Forsaken as they seek to secure northern Silverpine Forest.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:pyrewood-village":[["subzone:pyrewood-village#section2","The Moonrage Curse","Archmage Arugal unleashed otherworldly beasts from Shadowfang Keep and cursed Pyrewood's living residents. By day, they look like ordinary human villagers and keep their normal faculties as they tend the town hall, blacksmith, and chapel. The village merchants even trade with visiting Alliance travelers. When the sun sets, every resident becomes a savage, bloodthirsty worgen of the Moonrage pack. Anyone left within the village walls is attacked.",["q421.5"]]],"subzone:ragefire-chasm":[["subzone:ragefire-chasm#section2","The Searing Blade","The Searing Blade works from the shadows of Ragefire Chasm. Directed by the warlock Jergosh the Invoker, the satyr Bazzalan, and the towering felguard Taragaman the Hungerer, the sect plots to ruin Thrall’s Horde. Neeru Fireblade claims to oppose them, but the cult tests its strength and sabotages the orcs’ new home from below.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:ragefire-chasm-classic":[["subzone:ragefire-chasm-classic#section2","The Searing Blade","The Searing Blade works beneath Orgrimmar in service to the demonic Shadow Council. Taragaman the Hungerer, a felguard, leads the cult with Jergosh the Invoker, an orc warlock, and Bazzalan, a satyr. They seek to undo all that Thrall's Horde has established in Durotar. Warchief Thrall keeps watch on their activities, hoping their overconfidence will expose higher leaders within the Shadow Council.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:roland-s-doom":[["subzone:roland-s-doom#section2","The Fall of Velinde Starsong","Deep in Roland's Doom, the night elven priestess Velinde Starsong met her end while carrying the Scythe of Elune, an ancient artifact of immense and volatile power. Disturbed earth in the mine can stir Velinde's lingering spirit, who speaks of her final moments. Later, the terrified wanderer Jitters found the scythe inside the mine. His accidental release of more savage worgen drew the deadly Dark Riders to Duskwood.",["q1022.6"]]],"subzone:thandol-span":[["subzone:thandol-span#section2","A Lifeline Severed","Constructed following the War of the Three Hammers to bridge Ironforge with the Wildhammer clan of the Aerie Peak, the Thandol Span stands among the grandest stonecraft feats of dwarven history. Capable of supporting entire marching armies, it served as the critical land corridor between Lordaeron and Khaz Modan throughout the Second War, notably carrying Sir Anduin Lothar's counter-offensive southward. Today, the crossing is grievously wounded. Dark Iron saboteurs led by Balgaras the Foul and Kam Deepfury mounted a devastating assault from nearby Dun Modr, shattering one span entirely with explosives and threatening to sever overland travel between the allied realms completely.",["q303.3"]]],"subzone:tower-of-althalaxx":[["subzone:tower-of-althalaxx#section2","The Dark Strand","The Tower of Althalaxx stirs with malignant sorcery. Warlocks, dark cultists calling themselves the Cult of the Dark Strand, and allied satyrs have gathered in the ruin under Athrikus Narassin's leadership. Their rites channel demonic magic and torment the lingering spirits of the ancient dead, serving sinister designs tied directly to the demonic Shadow Council.",["q965.3"]]],"subzone:valley-of-trials":[["subzone:valley-of-trials#section2","The Shadow in the North","The northern caves have brought a new danger to the Valley of Trials. Cultists of the Burning Blade have settled in the natural caves along the cliffs and summon minor demonic familiars into the valley. Trainers use the intruders to give fresh recruits live combat practice. But fel magic so close to Orgrimmar’s threshold fuels rumors that the Shadow Council is trying to regain its hold over the orcish people.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:wailing-caverns":[["subzone:wailing-caverns#section1","The Druids of the Fang","Naralex's followers guard him as he sleeps in the Wailing Caverns. His closest disciples were driven mad by the Emerald Nightmare and abandoned their old traditions. Under leaders such as Lady Anacondra, they formed the Druids of the Fang and took on venomous snake aspects. They defend their master from anyone who might break his trance. The waterways now threaten to poison oases across the Barrens. Cenarion Circle allies in Thunder Bluff are concerned, while goblins in Ratchet see an opportunity.",["q886.5","q886.6"]],["subzone:wailing-caverns#section2","The Dream Curdled","Steam bursts from underground geysers and escapes through cracks in the rock, sending mournful wails through the caverns. The night elf druid Naralex entered these deep waterways with his faithful followers. He hoped to draw on the subterranean springs and channel the Emerald Dream, bringing bloom to the barren savannas. Instead, the Emerald Nightmare trapped his consciousness. Corruption seeps from the sleeping druid, warping the springs and changing local beasts into deviate horrors.",["q886.5"]]],"topic:burning-blade-clan":[["topic:burning-blade-clan#section3","The Traitor in the Cleft","While Thrall works to establish the New Horde, the cult's master orchestrator sits directly under his nose. Neeru Fireblade, an ostensible Horde warlock dwelling in the Cleft of Shadow, secretly leads the Burning Blade as an agent of the Shadow Council. Neeru uses pawns, including the expendable Searing Blade of Ragefire Chasm, to maintain his disguise as a benign researcher helping the Warchief. Unbeknownst to Neeru, Thrall has already deduced his treacherous allegiance and watches him closely.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]],["topic:burning-blade-clan#section4","A Split Legacy","Following the Second War, the surviving remnants of the Burning Blade split dramatically. A disciplined contingent of blademasters swore solemn oaths to liberate their people from demonic enslavement, pledging their blades directly to Warchief Thrall as his personal honor guard. Yet a far larger portion of the scattered clan fell entirely under the sway of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, morphing into a clandestine cult of warlocks, fel acolytes, and conspirators lurking throughout Kalimdor.",["q5726.3"]]],"topic:dark-riders":[["topic:dark-riders#section3","Shadows over Duskwood","Whispers in Duskwood link the Riders' sinister hunts to the rogue necromancer Morbent Fel, though no one knows what binds the two together, or who the 'Lord' the Riders answer to may be.",["q164.5"]]],"topic:defias-brotherhood":[["topic:defias-brotherhood#section1","A Web of Conspiracies","The Brotherhood has long outgrown a simple band of displaced laborers. VanCleef recruited mercenary goblins to oversee armaments, struck alliances with Riverpaw gnolls and Elwynn kobolds, and traded with cutthroat pirates like the Bloodsail Buccaneers. Defias highwaymen raid gold shipments across Elwynn Forest, and their agents even established a foothold in Duskwood before unearthing a cursed artifact in Roland's Doom. Far worse, the Brotherhood's influence reaches into the very heart of the capital, enjoying covert support from treacherous aristocrats within the House of Nobles while orchestrating kidnappings that strike at the highest offices of the Alliance.",["unsent.lescovar"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section2","The Juggernaught in the Deadmines","Beneath Moonbrook lies the Ironclad Cove, where VanCleef's goblin engineers are secretly assembling an enormous iron-plated warship known as the Defias Juggernaught. The guildmaster intends to sail this floating fortress out through the subterranean coastal waterways and bombard Stormwind City from the sea, reducing his own grand architecture to rubble. The conspiracy extends into the royal court itself: corrupt nobles like Lord Gregor Lescovar serve as eyes and ears for the uprising, while the court machinations of Lady Katrana Prestor deliberately keep the kingdom fractured and blind to the rising fleet beneath Westfall.",["defias.juggernaut","unsent.lescovar","prestor.onyxia"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section3","From Masons to Marauders","Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild was hired to rebuild Stormwind from smoldering ruins into a grand stone capital. When their masterwork was finished, the House of Nobles refused to deliver the promised payment, citing crushing wartime debt. Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef spoke out on behalf of his workers, demanding fair restitution, but the nobles responded by ordering the Stonemasons Guild disbanded. The ensuing riots in the city streets ended with the laborers driven out of Stormwind into exile. Stripped of their livelihood and outlawed, VanCleef and the desperate workers banded together, binding their faces in red silk to represent the blood of the wronged, and turned to highway robbery to claim what they believed they were owed.",["defias.masons"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section4","The Shadow Over Westfall","With the royal army overextended, VanCleef turned the neglected farmlands of Westfall into the Brotherhood's base of operations. The bandits overran the hamlet of Moonbrook and commandeered the abandoned subterranean tunnels of the Deadmines. Defias agents seized local mines, driven farmers from their homesteads with reprogrammed harvest golems, and salted the earth to starve the region. With the Crown unable or unwilling to send soldiers to protect rural citizens, local farmers formed The People's Militia out of Sentinel Hill to fight a desperate war of attrition against an enemy that now outmans and outguns them.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:druids-of-the-fang":[["topic:druids-of-the-fang#section2","Descent into Nightmare","Something went horribly awry inside Naralex's trance. Instead of channeling the pristine serenity of the Emerald Dream, Naralex’s slumber was infiltrated by a malevolent, twisting nightmare. This darkness seeped outwards through the caverns, rapidly warping the resident wildlife and poisoning the underground springs. Rather than fleeing to seek aid from the Cenarion Circle, Naralex's acolytes were overwhelmed by the nightmare's influence, their minds and spirits mutating alongside the subterranean ecosystem.",["q886.5"]]],"topic:gnoll":[["topic:gnoll#section4","Pestilence and Territory","Gnoll menace is not limited to the human south. In northern Lordaeron, the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw raises undead Rot Hide gnolls to dig up corpses across Tirisfal and Silverpine. Near Hillsbrad, the Mudsnout harvest foul, toxic blossoms among ruined farmsteads. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, the Palemane pack butchers game across Mulgore despite tauren efforts to make peace, while the Woodpaw of Feralas wage a desperate, frenzied war against encroaching silithid swarms.",["q437.6"]]],"topic:goblin":[["topic:goblin#section3","Hired Hands and Industry","A goblin's primary loyalty belongs to gold, and many operate outside the neutral cartels as hired specialists or ruthless corporate barons. The Venture Company pursues unchecked deforestation and resource stripping across continents, heedless of local populations. Elsewhere, skilled artificers sell their talents directly to criminal enterprises: deep inside the Deadmines, goblin engineers like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin oversee smelters and shipwrights constructing a massive ironclad juggernaut for the Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:grimtotem-tribe":[["topic:grimtotem-tribe#section3","Shadows and Apothecaries","Beneath Magatha's veneer of spiritual counsel sits an intricate web of intrigue. The Elder Crone is an ardent sponsor of the tauren's controversial ties with the Forsaken, publicly claiming she wishes to heal the undead, while secretly seeking out toxic plagues and Highborne artifacts. In Thousand Needles, scouts from Freewind Post intercept guarded correspondence between cloaked couriers and Grimtotem chieftains in Darkcloud Pinnacle.",["q4542.4"]]],"topic:house-of-nobles":[["topic:house-of-nobles#section3","Whispers of Treason","The council's obstinacy is far from simple greed. In truth, Lady Katrana Prestor—secretly the black dragon Onyxia—has systematically manipulated the House of Nobles to paralyze the kingdom. By stoking the aristocracy's arrogance during the stonemason negotiations and encouraging them to hoard royal funds, she orchestrated the realm's internal paralysis. High-ranking aristocrats like Lord Gregor Lescovar actively conspire alongside the Defias Brotherhood in the shadow of the throne, ensuring no imperial troops march to save the outlying towns.",["q65.7"]],["topic:house-of-nobles#section4","The Labor Revolt","Following the Second War, the House of Nobles contracted Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef and the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild Stormwind City from its ashes. However, the nobility simultaneously incurred staggering debts while expanding the kingdom's military frontier south into Stranglethorn. When the gleaming capital was finished, the nobles balked at the invoice and refused to pay the workers their full wages. Enraged by the betrayal, the masons rioted in the streets, prompting the nobility to banish the guild, driving the ruined workers into Westfall to form the Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.masons"]]],"topic:juggernaught":[["topic:juggernaught#section2","Ironclad Cove","Hidden beneath the hills of Westfall, deep within the subterranean caverns of the Deadmines, lies Ironclad Cove. There, secluded away from prying eyes and royal inspectors, Edwin VanCleef's craftsmen labored secretly to construct a fully armed juggernaught. By assembling a heavy warship in an underground cavern connected directly to the open sea, the Defias planned to bypass Stormwind's overland defenses and launch a surprise naval strike right into the heart of the kingdom.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:stonemasons-guild":[["topic:stonemasons-guild#section3","Blood in the Streets","The wage dispute erupted into violent unrest throughout Stormwind. The Crown cracked down ruthlessly, capturing key conspirators like VanCleef's lieutenant, Bazil Thredd, while VanCleef and hundreds of ruined stonemasons fled into the countryside of Westfall. Exiled and branded as traitors, the desperate artisans transformed their trade union into the militant Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.masons"]]],"topic:stormwind-army":[["topic:stormwind-army#section3","A Thin Blue Line","Despite its proud traditions, the modern army is stretched beyond its breaking point. Debts incurred by the House of Nobles to expand military reach into Stranglethorn fueled the bitter rift with the Stonemasons Guild, culminating in civil riots and the birth of the Defias Brotherhood. With King Varian missing, the House of Nobles has routinely withheld troops, funding, and supplies from outlying settlements like Westfall, Lakeshire, and Duskwood, forcing local marshals to deputize wandering adventurers to carry the realm's burdens.",["q123.3"]]],"topic:westfall-brigade":[["topic:westfall-brigade#section3","Uncovering the Conspiracy","The People's Militia learns the scale of the Defias threat through scouting and outside help. The Brotherhood is more than a band of country thieves: it operates under Edwin VanCleef, the former Guildmaster of the Stonemasons who rebuilt Stormwind. From the Deadmines beneath Moonbrook, the Defias plan to overthrow the kingdom. The embattled Militia is the only barrier between the capital and open insurrection.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"zone:barrens":[["zone:barrens#section3","The Emerald Corruption","Several oases break up the northern plains, their waters surging with unnatural vigor. The source lies in the Wailing Caverns, where the night elf druid Naralex tried to reach the Emerald Dream and restore the Barrens to its ancient forested state. A nightmare twisted his vision. Now corrupted water flows through the Forgotten Pools and Stagnant Oasis, giving rise to strange deviate plants, unnatural beasts, and the rogue Druids of the Fang.",["q886.5","q886.6"]]],"zone:deadmines":[["zone:deadmines#section3","The Secret of Ironclad Cove","Beyond the sweltering Goblin Foundry and timber-strewn Mast Room, Ironclad Cove opens beneath the Dagger Hills. In this hidden harbor, VanCleef employed goblin shipbuilders and tinkerers to construct a colossal ironclad dreadnaught. Captain Greenskin commands the warship, with First Mate Mr. Smite beside him. It is designed to sail through an underwater sea gate toward the ocean, where the Defias Brotherhood means to prey on the sea trade that keeps Stormwind rich.",["defias.juggernaut"]]],"zone:duskwood":[["zone:duskwood#section2","The Scythe and the Wolf-Men","Night elf priestess Velinde Starsong brought the Scythe of Elune to Duskwood. After she vanished in the disused mine called Roland's Doom, prospecting laborer Jitters eventually unearthed the relic. Careless handling of the weapon unleashed worgen, ravenous bipedal wolf-beasts from beyond this world. Shrouded in perpetual mist and bloodlust, these predators tore through the western groves, adding to the misery of a region already burdened by undead curses.",["q1022.6"]]],"zone:elwynn":[["zone:elwynn#section3","The Red Bandana Threat","The Defias Brotherhood has spread through Elwynn as an organized band of outlaws. Its members came from Edwin VanCleef’s Stonemasons Guild after bitter disputes over pay with the House of Nobles. They have taken the Northshire Vineyards, the Brackwell Pumpkin Patch, Jerod’s Landing, and Mirror Lake Orchard. Rumors say their agents are guiding nearby gnolls and kobolds to unsettle the realm. Guards Rolf and Malakai went to investigate murlocs near Stone Cairn Lake; both were killed and left on its banks.",["defias.masons"]]],"zone:hallofthanes":[["zone:hallofthanes#section3","The Threat of Dirgehammer","Within the heart of the complex lies a formidable assembly of threats, from enchanted guardians like Plunder and the fiery brute Magmatus to rogue figures of ancient dwarven lineage such as Faldrim Anvilmar. Presiding over the inner sanctuary is Durgen Dirgehammer, whose defeat finally quells the growing unrest simmering beneath the city floor and secures the sacred dwarven halls from subversion.",["q96392.4"]]],"zone:ragefire":[["zone:ragefire#section3","The Warchief's Game","Neeru Fireblade appears to be a warlock trainer beside the chasm's entrance in the Cleft of Shadow. In secret, he leads the local Burning Blade. Neeru sends Horde adventurers into the depths as loyalty tests, hoping to cull weak cultists and kill Thrall's supporters. Thrall knows of Neeru's deception. The Warchief uses these forays to eliminate dangerous cult leaders such as Jergosh and Bazzalan, while keeping Neeru in place to watch the Shadow Council's broader schemes.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]]],"zone:silverpine":[["zone:silverpine#section2","Rival Enclaves and the Scourge","The Forsaken's claim to Silverpine faces challenges from living and undead factions. On Fenris Isle to the east, Thule Ravenclaw leads Scourge remnants and commands plague-addled Rot Hide gnolls that trouble the northern farmlands. In the south, human mages from Dalaran hold the fortified settlement of Ambermill, where they conduct mystical research and oppose Forsaken expansion. At the Sepulcher, the Royal Apothecary Society and High Executor Hadrec wage a brutal campaign against these threats while testing virulent new plagues.",["q437.6"]],["zone:silverpine#section3","The Worgen and Shadowfang","Shadowfang Keep looms above Silverpine's southwest woods, held by the mad Dalaran archmage Arugal. The keep was once Baron Silverlaine's seat. During the Third War, Arugal summoned wolf-beasts from another dimension to repel the Scourge. The beasts turned on their summoners and spread a lycanthropic curse through the southern woods. By daylight, human villagers in Pyrewood Village appear ordinary and go about their chores. Beneath the night sky, they transform into savage worgen.",["q421.5"]]],"zone:stockade":[["zone:stockade#section2","Nobles and Assassins","The riot exposed Stormwind's political dealings. Outside territories had grown frustrated with the House of Nobles halting executions for its own leverage. Redridge guard Berton wanted the captured orc Targorr the Dread executed for atrocities, but the nobles froze his sentence. Darkshire councilman Millstipe saw grave robber Dextren Ward spared the gallows despite desecrating graves for the necromancer Morbent Fel. The dwarves of Dun Modr also demanded the death of Dark Iron saboteur Kam Deepfury. With the Stockade in chaos, local authorities quietly hired adventurers to carry out the executions the capital's courts refused to enforce.",["q303.3"]]],"zone:stormwind":[["zone:stormwind#section4","Where the Masons Went","The stonemasons gathered again in Westfall under their guildmaster, Edwin VanCleef. There they became the Defias Brotherhood, red-masked bandits now strangling the kingdom’s farmland. The builders of Stormwind’s white walls have sworn to make the House of Nobles pay for every stone.",["defias.masons"]],["zone:stormwind#section5","A Friend at Court","The Defias Brotherhood found help within Stormwind Keep. Lord Gregor Lescovar of the House of Nobles secretly dealt with the Brotherhood, passing along the kingdom’s plans while sitting among the lords who had cheated the stonemasons.",["unsent.lescovar"]],["zone:stormwind#section6","Shadows in the Keep","Lady Katrana Prestor has secretly guided the House of Nobles’ decisions. She inflamed its conflict with the stonemasons and ignored pleas for military aid from settlements such as Westfall and Redridge. She also whispered poison to Highlord Bolvar, keeping Stormwind’s legions idle and divided as unrest beyond the city walls threatens the throne.",["prestor.onyxia"]]],"zone:westfall":[["zone:westfall#section4","Masons in Red Masks","Edwin VanCleef leads the Brotherhood. He was the guildmaster who rebuilt Stormwind City, but the House of Nobles denied his stonemasons their pay, and riots drove them out. They took up the red mask in Westfall. The bandits burning its fields are the same hands that raised the capital's walls.",["defias.masons"]],["zone:westfall#section5","The Deadmines Conspiracy","Beneath Moonbrook, Edwin VanCleef has gathered cutthroats, goblins, and renegades in the abandoned gold shafts of the Deadmines. The Brotherhood is building a massive ironclad juggernaut there, meant to sail up the coast and bombard Stormwind City.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout","defias.juggernaut"]]]};
